  🔴 THE INSTRUMENT, AND WHY IT IS DIFFERENT FROM T43858's.  Both host NICs land on the DUT,
  on the two segments this case isolates from each other.  So the far-side count is a REAL
  far-side count: frames are crafted on eth3 and **counted as they arrive on eth4**, already
  routed and L2-rewritten by the DUT.  `arpleg.py --watch eth4` captures there, and
  `--answer 192.168.48.2=... --answer-iface eth3,eth4` runs a background ARP responder on eth4
  so the DUT can resolve the destination (this DUT does not learn a neighbour from a request it
  merely answers -- group A).  Sender is always 192.168.10.5 / 00:11:22:33:44:01 on eth3, next
  hop e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43.  No host networking is changed at any point; 192.168.x is a fiction.

    192.168.48.2   SUBJECT.  A fictitious host on the vlan48 segment.  **Counted on eth4.**
                   This is the cross-VRF flow the whole case turns on.
    192.168.48.1   SUBJECT-2.  The DUT's OWN vlan48 address -- i.e. "a Layer 3 interface in
                   another VRF domain on the same device", which is exactly what G2 says must
                   become unreachable.  Counted as echo replies on eth3.
    192.168.48.3   SUBJECT-3 (steps 6c/6d only).  A second host on the same /24 that has NEVER
                   been forwarded to.  It is what separates "the route is gone" from "one
                   programmed host entry is stale".
    203.0.113.9    CONTROL.  No route ever exists for it, in any VRF.  0 in every leg.
    192.168.10.1   INSTRUMENT CONTROL.  The DUT's own vlan10 address -- the SAME VRF as the
                   sender, so it must be answered in EVERY leg.  A zero elsewhere is then a
                   measurement and not a dead instrument.


