Objective: the identical frames that crossed in step 1 must now not cross, and the DUT's own
L3 interface in the other VRF must become unreachable -- with the instrument proven alive.

  Leg V2-vrf-isolated, SAME tool invocation as step 1, only the tag changed.
  cap V2-...-eth3.pcap: 29 frames.   cap **V2-...-eth4.pcap: 0 frames**

    192.168.48.2 (SUBJECT, counted on eth4) : 5 sent, **0 ARRIVED ON eth4**   (5/5 in step 1)
    192.168.48.1 (SUBJECT-2)                : 5 sent, **0 echo replies**      (5/5 in step 1)
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)                  : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT)               : 5 sent, **5 echo replies**  -- the vlan10 SVI is
                   alive and routing INSIDE VRF red, so both zeros above are measurements.

  The eth4 capture is **empty -- zero frames, not a low count**.  Nothing at all crossed.
  The DUT also returned 2 ICMP type 3; decoded from the pcap they are
    `ICMP host 192.168.48.2 unreachable` (code 1, not code 0)
  i.e. the DUT made and reported a routing decision about the cross-VRF destination
  specifically.  (Generation is rate-limited -- platforms/x960.md 7.8 -- so 2 of 15 is the
  expected shape and the count carries no meaning; the DESTINATION named inside them does.)

  ⇒ **G2 and G4 CONFIRMED on the wire**: with no configuration other than VRF membership, a
    frame from one VRF neither reaches a host in the other VRF's segment nor the other VRF's
    own L3 interface on the same device.

  STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS.


STEP 4 -- THE DECISIVE CONTROL: SAME PORTS, SAME ADDRESSES, ONLY THE VRF CHANGES
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: prove the zero in step 3 was VRF isolation and not a broken port, a dead cable, an
STP block or a lost ARP.  The cleanest way is to put BOTH interfaces in the SAME VRF and send
the identical frames again -- nothing else whatsoever changes.

  🔴 FIRST, AN ATTEMPT THAT WAS REFUSED, AND IT IS A FINDING (23:26:41):
    awplus(config)# interface vlan48
    awplus(config-if)# ip vrf forwarding red          <- vlan48 is currently in blue
    **% vlan48: VRF is already associated**
  The guide (p.7) describes moving an interface "from one VRF instance to another via command"
  as a supported operation whose ifindex is preserved.  **On this build a DIRECT VRF-to-VRF
  move is refused**; the interface must be returned to the global domain first.  See F-8 below.

  The sequence that works (23:27:10):
    awplus(config-if)# no ip vrf forwarding blue
    **% All IP addresses configured on interface vlan48 have been removed**
    awplus(config-if)# do show running-config interface vlan48   ->  (nothing -- stanza empty)
    awplus(config-if)# ip vrf forwarding red
    awplus(config-if)# ip address 192.168.48.1/24

    awplus# show ip vrf
    red                     --                    lo1 / vlan10 / **vlan48**
    blue                    --                    lo2                       <- now empty of SVIs
    awplus# show ip route vrf red
    C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10
    C       192.168.48.0/24 is directly connected, vlan48
    awplus# show ip route   ->  [VRF: blue]  "No entries in route table"
  ⇒ **G1 CONFIRMED**: vlan48 appears under exactly one VRF, never two.

  Leg V3-both-in-red, identical tool invocation.
  cap eth3: 32 frames.   cap **eth4: 7 frames**
    192.168.48.2 (SUBJECT, on eth4) : 5 sent, **5 ARRIVED**   (0 in step 3)
    192.168.48.1 (SUBJECT-2)        : 5 sent, **5 echo replies** (0 in step 3)
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)          : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT)       : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  ⇒ Same two physical ports, same two VLANs, same two IP addresses, same crafted frames, same
    ARP responder.  **The only difference between 0/5 and 5/5 is which VRF vlan48 belongs to.**
    That is the whole claim of VRF-Lite, measured.

  Returned to the isolated state for the next step (23:28:2x): `no ip vrf forwarding red` /
  `ip vrf forwarding blue` / `ip address 192.168.48.1/24` on vlan48, and re-measured so that
