          192.168.48.3 : 5 sent, 0 arrived
          203.0.113.9  : 0 · 192.168.10.1 : 5/5
      (T33247 recorded `clear arp-cache vrf` as NOT EXERCISED because no VRF existed on the DUT
      at the time.  It is exercised here, and it works: it clears only that VRF's table.)

  ⇒ 🔴 **FINDING F-9.  Removing a static inter-VRF route removes it from running-config and
     from the RIB -- `% Network not in table` -- while the DUT KEEPS FORWARDING ACROSS THE VRF
     BOUNDARY to any destination whose ARP entry was resolved while the route existed.  A
     destination not previously forwarded to is correctly blocked, so this is a stale
     ARP-derived host forwarding entry, not a stale route.  `clear arp-cache vrf <name>` clears
     it at once.**
     VRF-Lite is sold as a *security* separation ("securely isolated", "security domains"), so
     a configuration change that appears to close a leak and does not is worth a developer's
     attention.  How long the entry would survive on its own was not measured (the ARP ageing
     timer would eventually remove it); it was still forwarding ~2 minutes and three legs after
     the route was removed.
     **This is a property of inter-VRF static route REMOVAL, not of VRF-Lite isolation itself**
     -- isolation from a cold start (step 3) is complete and immediate -- so it does not make
     this case a FAIL.  It is recorded as a finding for the Test Engineer.

  STEP 6 VERDICT: PASS for the removal of the configuration and the RIB entry; the dataplane
  residue is recorded as F-9 with its mechanism and its remedy both measured.


STEP 7 -- OVERLAPPING ADDRESSES IN TWO VRFs  (tests G3, the headline claim of VRF-Lite)
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: the same IP address, live on two interfaces at once, each in its own VRF -- which is
impossible in a single global table and is the reason VRF-Lite exists.

  Applied 23:35:19 -- vlan48 (in VRF blue) given VRF red's vlan10 address:
    awplus(config)# interface vlan48
    awplus(config-if)# no ip address 192.168.48.1/24
    awplus(config-if)# ip address 192.168.10.1/24
  **Accepted with no message of any kind.**

    awplus# show ip interface brief
    (global)      vlan41 10.0.41.1/30 · vlan61 10.0.61.1/30
    [VRF: red]    lo1 unassigned · vlan10 **192.168.10.1/24**
    [VRF: blue]   lo2 unassigned · vlan48 **192.168.10.1/24**
    awplus# show ip route
    [VRF: red]    C 192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, **vlan10**
    [VRF: blue]   C 192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, **vlan48**
  The same /32 address and the same /24 connected prefix exist twice, in two route tables.

  BUT A CONFIGURATION THAT IS ACCEPTED IS NOT A FEATURE THAT WORKS, so both were measured.

  Leg V7a-overlap-from-red  (--iface eth3 --watch eth4, sender 192.168.10.5):
    cap eth3: 27 frames.   cap **eth4: 0 frames**
      x5 ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.5
      x5 ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at **e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43**          <- red's vlan10 answers
      x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.5 -> 192.168.10.1 ; **x5 ICMP t0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5**
      x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.5 -> 203.0.113.9 (CONTROL, 0 replies)
      -> 5/5 ARP, 5/5 ICMP, and NOTHING on eth4.

  Leg V7b-overlap-from-blue  (--iface **eth4** --watch eth3, sender 192.168.10.6 on the other
  segment, chosen inside the target address's own subnet because this DUT answers ARP only for
  a sender in that subnet -- F-4 / platforms/x960.md 7.6):
    cap eth4: 29 frames.   cap **eth3: 0 frames**
      x5 ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.6
      x5 ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at **e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43**          <- blue's vlan48 answers
      x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.6 -> 192.168.10.1 ; **x5 ICMP t0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.6**
      x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.6 -> 203.0.113.9 (CONTROL, 0 replies)
      x1 ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.6 tell **192.168.10.1** -- the DUT uses 192.168.10.1 as its
          own identity on the BLUE side too
      -> 5/5 ARP, 5/5 ICMP, and NOTHING on eth3.

  ⇒ **G3 CONFIRMED on the wire.**  One address, answered independently on two different ports
    in two different VRFs, 10 ARP replies and 10 echo replies in total, with each leg's
    opposite-NIC capture EMPTY -- so the two instances never see each other.
  ⚠️ WORTH KNOWING: both instances answer with **the same MAC**, because all SVIs on this
    platform share one (platforms/x960.md 6.5).  So two hosts in two different VRFs see the
    identical IP and the identical MAC for their default gateway, and cannot tell from the wire
    that they are talking to different routing domains.  That is correct VRF-Lite behaviour on
    a one-MAC platform, but it is a thing to know before debugging a VRF deployment by MAC.

  Reverted (23:36:xx): `no ip address 192.168.10.1/24` / `ip address 192.168.48.1/24` on
  vlan48, verified `[VRF: blue] vlan48 192.168.48.1/24`.

  Per-device .cfg files saved at this point, with the case's setup applied (VRFs red and blue,
  vlan10 in red, vlan48 in blue):
    T43859/swi_d.cfg (149 config lines).  Its VRF lines read `ip vrf red 1` / `ip vrf blue 2`
    -- running-config writes back the automatically assigned LO interface numbers (G8).
    T43859/swi_b.cfg, T43859/swi_a.cfg -- saved for completeness with headers that say so;
    **neither device is touched by this case** (DUT + host NICs only).

  STEP 7 VERDICT: PASS.


