9. FINDINGS RAISED BY THIS CASE
------------------------------------------------------------
  F-8  **A direct VRF-to-VRF move of a Layer 3 interface is REFUSED on this build.**
       `interface vlan48` (in blue) / `ip vrf forwarding red` -> `% vlan48: VRF is already
       associated`.  The guide (p.7) describes moving an interface "from one VRF instance to
       another via command" as supported, with the ifindex preserved.  The working sequence is
       `no ip vrf forwarding <old>` -> `ip vrf forwarding <new>` -> re-apply the address.  A
       documentation/implementation mismatch; low severity, but it will stop a migration script
       dead, and with a stop-on-error driver it abandons the rest of the batch.
  F-9  🔴 **Removing a static inter-VRF route leaves the dataplane forwarding across the VRF
       boundary.**  After `no ip route vrf red 192.168.48.0/24 vlan48`, with the line gone from
       running-config and `show ip route vrf red 192.168.48.2` answering `% Network not in
       table`, 5 of 5 crafted frames still crossed from VRF red and arrived on the VRF blue
       segment -- reproduced in three successive legs over ~2 minutes.  MECHANISM, measured: a
       second host on the same /24 that had never been forwarded to was correctly blocked
       (0/5), so the route really is gone and what survives is a stale ARP-derived per-host
       forwarding entry programmed while the route existed.  REMEDY, measured:
       `clear arp-cache vrf blue` stops it immediately (0/5 for both hosts).
       **Security-relevant**, because VRF-Lite is documented as providing "securely isolated"
       routing domains and "security domains": an operator who removes an inter-VRF route to
       close a path will see the configuration and the route table agree that it is closed
       while traffic keeps flowing.  How long the entry survives unaided was NOT measured.
       **It is not a failure of VRF-Lite isolation** -- isolation from a cold start is complete
       and immediate (steps 3 and 8a) -- so this case is not a FAIL.  Wants a developer.
  O-8  (documentation) The guide's p.17 prose, "These static routes must specify both the
       egress VLAN and next hop IP address", overstates the requirement: the VLAN-only form
       `ip route vrf red 192.168.48.0/24 vlan48` is accepted and works, and the guide's own
       worked example on the same page uses exactly that form.
  CLOSED FROM AN EARLIER CASE  T33247 (Clear ARP) had to record the `vrf` form of
       `clear arp-cache` as NOT EXERCISED because the DUT had no VRF at the time.  It is
       exercised here: `clear arp-cache vrf blue` empties that VRF's ARP table and leaves the
       others alone, and `show arp` / `show arp vrf <name>` are both VRF aware.
  CONFIRMED FROM THE GUIDE, all on the wire: G1 (an interface belongs to exactly one VRF),
       G2 + G4 (no communication between VRFs by default, including to the other VRF's own L3
       interface), G3 (overlapping addresses), G5 (static inter-VRF routing), G6 (the global
       table shrinks), G7 (the address is unset on a VRF move; ARP entries are VRF aware),
       G8 (an LO interface is created automatically per VRF).

================================================================================
VERDICT: PASS -- VRF-Lite proven on hardware across fourteen counted legs, every cross-VRF
result being a real far-side frame count on the second host NIC rather than a ping or a `show`.
Isolation: identical crafted frames cross 5/5 in the global table and 0/5 once the two SVIs sit
in different VRFs, with the DUT's own interface in the other VRF also going 5/5 -> 0/5 and its
interface in the sender's VRF answering 5/5 in every leg; putting BOTH SVIs back in one VRF
restores 5/5 with nothing else changed, which attributes the zero to VRF membership and nothing
else.  Overlapping addresses: one address live and answering on two ports in two VRFs, 5/5 each
way, with each leg's opposite capture empty.  Static inter-VRF routing opens exactly the two
prefixes named and no more.  Removal returns configuration, route tables and dataplane to a
frame-for-frame match of the baseline, and the whole configuration survives a real 60 s reboot
and is still functional afterwards.  Teardown diffed CLEAN on all three devices.
Three findings recorded (F-8 the refused VRF-to-VRF move, F-9 the stale cross-VRF forwarding
after an inter-VRF route is removed, O-8 a documentation overstatement).  F-9 is
security-relevant and wants a developer, but it concerns the REMOVAL of a deliberate inter-VRF
route, not VRF-Lite's isolation, which was complete and immediate in every cold-start leg.
================================================================================
